    "summary": "Monster Truckin' > Other One > Half‑Step jam shines despite PA quirks",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a tight performance highlighted by an epic Truckin' → The Other One → Mississippi Half‑Step sequence that rolls into Dew. Jerry’s guitar recovers from an early chimey tone to clean, locked‑in playing, while Keith’s piano steadies after initial hiccups.",
    "review": "On October 27, 1972 at Veterans’ Memorial Hall the Dead served up a classic late‑summer set that is remembered chiefly for its monster second‑set jam. After a first set hampered by PA compression and hall echo, the band launched into a scorching Truckin' → The Other One → Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo run that rolls seamlessly into Morning Dew – a sequence fans still cite as a highlight of the era. Jerry’s lead work was initially flat‑toned by the sound issues but tightened up for the jam, delivering crisp licks and soaring solos. Keith Godchaux’s piano was spotty at the start of the show, yet after crew adjustments it settled into solid support throughout the high‑energy stretch. Bob Weir tossed in his trademark stage‑side humor warning the crowd off the piano riser, adding a light‑hearted moment before the band dove back into the music. While the matrix recording mixes audience ambience with a modest soundboard, it captures the vivid energy of that night better than any other source, making it the go‑to version for listeners.\n\nThe show’s overall vibe is one of a band shaking off early technical gremlins and locking in for a memorable second half. For Deadheads seeking the definitive performance of the famed Truckin' → Other One → Half‑Step excursion, this recording offers both the raw live atmosphere and enough clarity to appreciate each musician’s contribution.",
